content.strength_and_weaknesses:
Strengths
* I believe the proposed task is novel and the paper is the first demonstrating that emergent communication-driven representations are more useful for downstream RL tasks than other self-supervised techniques;
* The paper is clearly written. The motivation and setting are well stated, and the needed ablations are conducted;
* I particularly appreciate the experiments on generalization to new tasks and the different strong baselines that the authors considered. 

Weaknesses:
* The results of the SimCLR experiments are very puzzling. Authors argue that this may be due to the rich representation encoded by the SimCLR model. However, this does not seem very convincing as Speaker can learn to use only the needed information. Perhaps the image encoder model is not frozen and the useful weights are destroyed during fine-tuning? I would encourage authors to clarify this and, if it is the case, run an experiment where the SimCLR encoder is frozen; 
* The “Visual representation” metric is similar to the one proposed by (Dessi et al. 2021). This should be made clearer in the paper.

content.clarity,_quality,_novelty_and_reproducib:
* Clarity: the paper is clearly written;
* Novelty & Quality: both the task and results are novel, the experimental study is of high quality;
* Reproducibility: The set of hyperparameters is given. However, no mention of open-sourcing the code.
